WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.109
What “fair” means.

2
00:00:01.389 --> 00:00:06.211
Sooner or later someone asks: should we switch to the new model from another provider?

3
00:00:06.491 --> 00:00:10.224
It’s on top of a leaderboard, it’s cheaper, a competitor uses it.

4
00:00:10.504 --> 00:00:13.576
Lessons 7 and 8 gave you the measuring instruments.

5
00:00:13.856 --> 00:00:19.125
This lesson is about using them so the comparison measures the models and not your setup.

6
00:00:19.405 --> 00:00:21.368
Start by naming what you’re comparing.

7
00:00:21.648 --> 00:00:34.811
Users don’t experience a model; they experience a system: model plus prompt plus settings (reasoning effort, max output tokens, tools) plus harness (retries, parsing, how tool results are fed back).

8
00:00:35.091 --> 00:00:41.725
Two systems can share a model and behave very differently, and the question “is model X better than model Y?

9
00:00:42.005 --> 00:00:46.251
” only has an answer for specific configurations on a specific task.

10
00:00:46.531 --> 00:00:57.197
So the unit of comparison is a configuration, and the claim you’re entitled to make is “on our task, configuration A beats configuration B by this much, with this interval.

11
00:00:57.477 --> 00:00:57.775
”.

12
00:00:58.055 --> 00:00:59.506
Fairness has four parts.

13
00:00:59.786 --> 00:01:08.916
Same task and data: every candidate answers the same questions, graded by the same graders, so the comparison can be paired (lesson 7).

14
00:01:09.196 --> 00:01:16.514
Equal effort: each candidate gets a comparable chance to be at its best, which is the subject of the next two sections.

15
00:01:16.794 --> 00:01:24.580
Same conditions: same time window, same region where possible, the same retry and timeout rules, and pinned model ids.

16
00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:32.839
And a decision rule written down before you look: the quality bar, the cost and latency limits, and how big a difference must be to matter.

17
00:01:33.119 --> 00:01:42.634
The last one is easy to skip and does the most to keep you honest, because everyone, engineers included, finds reasons to prefer the result they hoped for.
